ace with no incumbency benefit for either side", "Headline coverage centers on down-ballot contests, not the presidential race"], "rationale": "Arkansas's 15-point Republican lean, with no incumbency on the ballot and no polls, ratings, or market pricing pointing the other way, leaves the Republican nominee a strong favorite.", "watch": "The first credible statewide presidential polls or a major shift in the national political environment could move the margin in either direction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Arkansas remains firmly Republican-leaning in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of roughly 15 points toward the GOP. The 2028 race is open because the incumbent president is term-limited, so neither party gains an incumbency advantage. No public polls of the state, expert ratings, or prediction-market pricing exist for this contest, so the forecast rests on the state's underlying partisan fundamentals.\n\nRecent headline coverage has focused on down-ballot races: the 2026 governor's contest, the 2nd District House race where Chris Jones has drawn national Democratic support and endorsement from Kamala Harris, and legislative special elections. None of that directly changes the presidential picture. With a 15-point Republican lean and no offsetting evidence, the Republican nominee is a strong favorite, though a large national wave could narrow the margin somewhat.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0496, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0891, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0911, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0924, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0837, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0872, "m